```\
\
---\
\
# Interpretation\
\
## FP \'97 Field / Potential\
\
The field contains possible states.\
\
No preferred direction exists yet.\
\
Examples\
\
- Binary Grid\
- Trinary Grid\
- Angle Field\
- QR Matrix\
- Random Field\
- Structured Field\
\
Possible measurements\
\
- Entropy\
- State distribution\
- Local variance\
- Symmetry\
- Spatial autocorrelation\
\
---\
\
## RR \'97 Relation / Resonance\
\
Relations connect states.\
\
The relation layer transforms isolated points into a network.\
\
Possible relation rules\
\
- Neighbor\
- Similarity\
- Distance\
- Angle\
- Phase\
- Weight\
- Custom operators\
\
Outputs\
\
Weighted graph\
\
```\
G = (V,E,W)\
```\
\
Possible measurements\
\
- Degree\
- Connectivity\
- Clustering\
- Path Length\
- Bridges\
- Coherence\
- Temporal Stability\
\
---\
\
## OE \'97 Orientation / Emergence\
\
Orientation is the measurable structure emerging from relations.\
\
Possible measurements\
\
```\
Q\'b0 =\
(\
C,\
D,\
P,\
S,\
N\
)\
```\
\
Example interpretation\
\
C = Coherence\
\
D = Direction\
\
P = Persistence\
\
S = Stability\
\
N = Navigability\
\
Other possible metrics may emerge during implementation.\
\
---\
\
# Research Hypothesis\
\
H1\
\
> Stable orientation emerges primarily through relational structure (RR), not through field distribution (FP) alone.\
\
---\
\
# Null Hypothesis\
\
H0\
\
After controlling for field distribution,\
\
relation structure provides no additional explanatory power for orientation.\
\
